window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][12] = "<h1>Part III — Product Sense</h1>\n<span id=\"c07\"></span>\n<h2>C07 — Product Design: Designing for a User</h2>\n<span id=\"c07-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>This is one of the most heavily weighted question types in product loops <code>Inferred</code>, so the chapter is long and specific. The prompt is open-ended: &quot;Design a product for X,&quot; &quot;Design a feature for Y on a smartwatch,&quot; &quot;Design something to help people do Z.&quot; There is no right answer. The interviewer is watching how you get from a vague prompt to a defensible design, and whether you can show your reasoning at each step.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code> from the round structures candidates commonly report: a dedicated product sense or product design round at Google, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon, Apple and other companies, often 35 to 45 minutes, usually with a prompt tied to the company's own products or to a user group. On the job the same skill is what a PM does when a team has an area to work on and no solution yet: pick a user, find the problem, shape a solution, define success.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> Google publicly describes hiring on general cognitive ability, role-related knowledge, leadership and &quot;Googleyness&quot; <code>Public</code> <code>[V: summaries citing Google's how-we-hire page; Google re:Work structured-interviewing guide]</code>, and a design round shows the first two. Amazon publishes Customer Obsession as a leadership principle <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon's Leadership Principles page, amazon.jobs]</code>. Apple publishes platform design guidelines and a public privacy stance <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Apple Human Interface Guidelines and privacy pages]</code>, and third-party guides report that its loops weigh craft and fit with the ecosystem <code>Inferred</code>. Microsoft publishes inclusive-design principles <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Microsoft Inclusive Design toolkit manual]</code>. Scorers typically write about five things <code>Inferred</code>: whether you structured the answer, how well you understood and chose a user, whether the problem you chose was real and important, whether your solutions were creative and feasible, and whether you reached a decision and a way to measure it.</p>\n<p>Five observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You clarify the prompt and set a goal before you design.</li>\n<li>You choose a specific user by a stated criterion.</li>\n<li>You rank the user's problems and pick one with a reason.</li>\n<li>You offer solutions that differ in kind, choose one, and describe the first version.</li>\n<li>You define success, name the risks (including misuse and privacy), and say what would change your mind.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Six patterns end product design answers: a feature list with no user, a solution chosen before the problem, a demographic user (&quot;busy moms&quot;), solutions that are variations of one idea, no decision at the end, and ignoring the downside (privacy, safety, misuse, accessibility).</p>\n<span id=\"c07-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c07-c07-1-what-product-sense-is\"></span>\n<h4>C07.1 What product sense is</h4>\n<p><strong>Product sense</strong> is the ability to identify the right user problem, generate and choose between solutions, and define success, usually assessed with an open-ended prompt. <a href=\"#gl-product-sense\">product sense</a> is not taste alone, and it is not design in the sense of drawing screens. It is the chain of judgments that connects a person's situation to a thing worth building: who, what is hard for them, which of several ideas is best and why, and how you would know it worked.</p>\n<p>It builds on the earlier chapters. The decision structure and tradeoff vocabulary come from C02 (<a href=\"#gl-tradeoff\">tradeoff</a>, <a href=\"#gl-reversible-decision\">reversible decision</a>). The structuring habits come from C03 (<a href=\"#gl-mece\">MECE</a>, the issue tree). The metrics step is C05. The user understanding is C06 (<a href=\"#gl-user-segment\">user segment</a>, <a href=\"#gl-jobs-to-be-done\">jobs to be done</a>, the <a href=\"#gl-problem-statement\">problem statement</a>). This chapter assembles them into one answer you can deliver in 45 minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Why a fixed spine works.</strong> An open prompt invites wandering. A spine gives you somewhere to go next at every moment, and it makes your reasoning visible, which is what the interviewer can score. Candidates who improvise often produce good ideas that the interviewer cannot trace to a user or a problem.</p>\n<p><strong>What interviewers do not need.</strong> Wireframes, polished screens, or technical architecture. A rough sketch of a flow, described in words, is enough. Interviewers want product thinking, not interface design <code>Inferred</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use the full spine.</strong> If the prompt already fixes the user (&quot;design an alarm clock for people who are deaf&quot;), skip the segmentation and spend the time on the problem and the solutions. If the interviewer redirects you (&quot;let's go straight to solutions&quot;), follow them. If the prompt is about improving an existing product, the spine changes (C08), and for a market you do not know, it changes again (C09).</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> A candidate asked to &quot;design an app for people who are blind to get around a city&quot; spends twelve minutes segmenting users and listing criteria, though the prompt already named the user group. The interviewer is left with five minutes for the problem, and the solution is thin. Spend segmentation time where the prompt leaves the user open. A fixed spine is a guide to what to cover, not a quota of minutes for each step.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hidden risks.</strong> A rigid spine can make your answer sound recited (<a href=\"#gl-framework-theater\">framework theater</a>, C03). The defense is to adapt each step to the prompt, to say what you are skipping and why, and to let a good insight change the plan. A second risk is creativity theater: a surprising idea with no link to the user's problem. Interviewers reward ideas that follow from the problem.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-c07-2-the-answer-architecture-for-a-45-minute-round\"></span>\n<h4>C07.2 The answer architecture for a 45-minute round</h4>\n<p>Section 4 sets out the full spine with time budgets. In brief: clarify, set the goal, choose a user, rank the problems, generate solutions, evaluate and recommend, measure, and check the risks. Each step produces something the interviewer can write down.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-c07-3-clarify-then-set-the-goal\"></span>\n<h4>C07.3 Clarify, then set the goal</h4>\n<p><strong>Clarifying questions</strong> (three to five, about two minutes) fix the scope and the stakes. The useful kinds:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>Why it matters</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Product and platform</td>\n<td>&quot;Is this a new product or a feature in an existing one? On which device?&quot;</td>\n<td>Constrains the solution space</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Company and goal</td>\n<td>&quot;Why does the company want this? Growth, engagement, revenue, a mission?&quot;</td>\n<td>Defines what a good answer optimizes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>User</td>\n<td>&quot;Is there a particular user in mind?&quot;</td>\n<td>Saves the segmentation step if one is given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Constraints</td>\n<td>&quot;Any limits on time, team, market or technology?&quot;</td>\n<td>Prevents an unbuildable answer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Success</td>\n<td>&quot;How will the team judge it?&quot;</td>\n<td>Seeds the metrics step</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>If the interviewer says &quot;you decide,&quot; state your assumptions aloud: &quot;I'll assume this is a new feature in an existing mobile app, with a goal of increasing meaningful use, and I'll flag if that changes.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Set the goal.</strong> Name what you are optimizing and for whom, in a sentence. A goal ladder (FW-C07-02) has three rungs: the company's purpose in building this, the product's goal in this area, and the user's goal. The best answers show that the user's goal and the company's goal point the same way, and say so when they do not. The goal also bounds the problem: &quot;increase the number of people who finish a first workout&quot; leads to different designs from &quot;increase revenue per subscriber.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> The goal is the criterion against which you will choose a solution later. Without it, every solution is as good as another.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to do it at length.</strong> In a short prompt, a one-sentence goal is enough.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-c07-4-selecting-a-user-segmentation-criteria-and-a-defensibl\"></span>\n<h4>C07.4 Selecting a user: segmentation criteria and a defensible choice</h4>\n<p>Apply C06.2 in a minute or two: list three or four segments by need and behavior, pick one by a stated criterion, and say what you give up. Four criteria cover most prompts: size, pain intensity, reach, and fit with the company (FW-C06-02).</p>\n<p>A short persona sentence helps the interviewer see the person: &quot;Maya, a first-time marathon trainer, follows a 16-week plan, runs before work, and checks her wrist mid-run.&quot; Keep it to two sentences, and make every detail tie to a need or constraint. Decorative details (a hobby, a pet) add nothing.</p>\n<p><strong>Name your criterion out loud.</strong> &quot;I'll focus on first-time trainers because their pain is most acute and they have the least support, and I'll leave experienced runners, who are a larger but better-served group.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>When the user is given.</strong> Confirm it and move on, and say what makes this user's situation specific.</p>\n<p><strong>Hidden risk.</strong> Picking a segment because it is easy to imagine rather than because it matters. A good check is whether the interviewer could argue for a different segment and whether you could answer them.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-c07-5-pain-points-ranking-by-frequency-and-severity\"></span>\n<h4>C07.5 Pain points: ranking by frequency and severity</h4>\n<p>A <strong>pain point</strong> is a specific, observable difficulty a user meets while trying to do something, which can be ranked by how often it occurs and how much it costs them. <a href=\"#gl-pain-point\">pain point</a> is the unit of the problem step. Generate pain points by walking through the user's journey in stages (before, during, after, or by steps of the task) and listing what goes wrong in each (FW-C07-03).</p>\n<p><strong>Rank them.</strong> Use frequency (how often), severity (how costly when it happens) and the current workaround (what they do about it; a workaround is evidence the pain is real). Pick the top one or two, say why, and say what you are leaving.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pain point</th>\n<th>Frequency</th>\n<th>Severity</th>\n<th>Workaround today</th>\n<th>Rank</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Make them specific.</strong> &quot;Running is hard&quot; is not a pain point. &quot;Going out too fast in the first third of a long run and fading&quot; is. A pain point should describe something you could watch happen.</p>\n<p><strong>Say how you know.</strong> In an interview you cannot run research, so say whether the ranking comes from experience, from reasoning about the journey, or from what you would verify (&quot;I'd check this with interviews, and I'd expect pacing to rank above shoe selection&quot;). That honesty reads as judgment, not weakness.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> The problem you choose bounds the design, and the ranking shows you made a choice and can defend it.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to rank at length.</strong> If the prompt names the problem (&quot;help people who forget to take medication&quot;), spend the time on why it happens and for whom, not on a long list.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> A candidate lists eight pain points for every user group, gives each one sentence, and ranks none. The interviewer cannot tell which problem the later solutions address. Fewer, ranked, and specific beats many and flat.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c07-c07-6-generating-solutions-that-differ-in-kind\"></span>\n<h4>C07.6 Generating solutions that differ in kind</h4>\n<p>Interviewers want to see range and judgment. Offer three or four solutions that differ <strong>in kind, not in degree</strong>: not &quot;a small widget, a bigger widget, a full app,&quot; but different ways of solving the problem. FW-C07-04 lists a spectrum:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Idea</th>\n<th>Example for &quot;runners go out too fast&quot;</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Eliminate</td>\n<td>Remove the cause of the pain</td>\n<td>A plan that sets pace automatically each mile</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Automate</td>\n<td>Do the work for the user</td>\n<td>The device adjusts targets from heart rate in real time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assist</td>\n<td>Give information or a nudge at the right moment</td>\n<td>A tap on the wrist when pace exceeds the target</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reframe</td>\n<td>Change what the user is trying to do</td>\n<td>Train by effort level rather than pace</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Connect</td>\n<td>Use other people or systems</td>\n<td>A friend paces with you remotely</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>For each, say in one or two sentences how it works for the user, why it addresses the pain, and what it costs. Do not describe screens. Describe moments: &quot;at mile three, her wrist taps once if she is 10% faster than her plan.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Use constraints as a source of ideas.</strong> The platform (a watch has seconds of attention), the context (hands busy), and the user's resources (no time to learn) all narrow and sharpen solutions.</p>\n<p><strong>Technology is a tool, not a solution.</strong> Mention machine learning or a new sensor only where it solves a named problem, and say what happens when it fails (C24 and C25 for AI features).</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Range shows you are not attached to the first idea, and the contrast makes your choice a judgment.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use many options.</strong> If time is short, two well-contrasted options are better than four thin ones.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-c07-7-evaluating-recommending-and-phasing\"></span>\n<h4>C07.7 Evaluating, recommending and phasing</h4>\n<p>Compare options against criteria drawn from your goal, using a short tradeoff table (FW-C02-03, FW-C07-05). Typical criteria: impact on the goal, effort, risk, reach, and fit with the company's strengths. Do not hide behind scores; say the sacrifice: &quot;I choose the haptic pacing, which gives up adaptive plans for the first version.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Recommend one.</strong> Describe the first version in four to six steps as a flow of moments, not as screens: what the user does, what the product does, and what the user feels. Then say what comes next: a second version and a third, in order, with the reason for the order.</p>\n<p><strong>Say what would change your mind.</strong> Name a result that would make you pick a different option (C02's closing habit).</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> A recommendation with a stated cost shows decision-making, which is the signal that separates strong from baseline answers.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to phase.</strong> If the interviewer asks only for the idea, a sentence on what comes later is enough.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-c07-8-success-metrics-and-guardrails\"></span>\n<h4>C07.8 Success metrics and guardrails</h4>\n<p>Use the template from C05 (FW-C05-06): the goal, the user behavior, one primary metric defined precisely, two or three inputs, guardrails with thresholds, a counter metric, and how you would measure. In a design round you have about five minutes, so be compact: one primary metric with a definition, one input, one guardrail, one counter metric, and a sentence on how you would learn whether the design causes the effect.</p>\n<p>Match the metric to the problem you chose, not to a generic metric. A pacing feature is measured by completed runs at planned pace, not by app opens.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-c07-9-the-responsible-design-check-trust-safety-privacy-misu\"></span>\n<h4>C07.9 The responsible-design check: trust, safety, privacy, misuse</h4>\n<p>Every product can hurt someone. End your answer by saying how this one could, and what you would do about it. FW-C07-06 gives a checklist. The questions that matter most:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Misuse:</strong> how could a bad actor use this against another person? (Location, messaging and sharing features need this question first.)</li>\n<li><strong>Vulnerable users:</strong> children, older adults, people in crisis or in unsafe relationships.</li>\n<li><strong>Privacy and consent:</strong> what data does this use, who sees it, can the person say no, and is the default the protective one?</li>\n<li><strong>Safety:</strong> could the design lead to physical harm (a distracted walker, a runner pushed beyond a safe effort)?</li>\n<li><strong>Wellbeing:</strong> could it encourage compulsive use or anxiety?</li>\n<li><strong>Accessibility and inclusion:</strong> who cannot use it as designed?</li>\n<li><strong>Failure:</strong> what happens when it is wrong (a wrong alert, a missed signal)?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>State two or three that matter for this product, with a concrete design response (&quot;sharing is off until each person opts in, with a visible indicator and a one-tap pause&quot;). Naming the risk without a response is half an answer, and a risk list with no link to the design is filler.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> It shows maturity, and at several companies it is a stated value (privacy at Apple, inclusive design at Microsoft, trust and integrity questions at social companies <code>Inferred</code>).</p>\n<p><strong>When not to dwell.</strong> Do not let it consume the answer; two minutes at the end is right.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-c07-10-designing-across-surfaces-and-form-factors-including-\"></span>\n<h4>C07.10 Designing across surfaces and form factors, including hardware-software integration</h4>\n<p>The same problem leads to different designs on different surfaces. The surface decides how much attention you have, how the user inputs, and what the product can sense. FW-C07-07 gives the lens.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Surface</th>\n<th>Attention</th>\n<th>Input</th>\n<th>Typical strengths</th>\n<th>Typical limits</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Phone</td>\n<td>Focused, short sessions</td>\n<td>Touch, voice</td>\n<td>Rich interaction; always with the user</td>\n<td>Hands and eyes busy in motion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Watch</td>\n<td>Glance of a few seconds</td>\n<td>Taps, crown or buttons, voice</td>\n<td>On the body; sensors; haptics</td>\n<td>Tiny screen; battery; little text</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Voice speaker or assistant</td>\n<td>Ambient, eyes free</td>\n<td>Speech</td>\n<td>Hands-free</td>\n<td>No screen; errors in noise; privacy at home</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TV</td>\n<td>Shared, leaned back</td>\n<td>Remote</td>\n<td>Group viewing; big screen</td>\n<td>Slow input; poor for reading</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Car</td>\n<td>Safety-critical</td>\n<td>Voice, controls</td>\n<td>Hands-free context</td>\n<td>Distraction risk; must be glanceable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Physical device</td>\n<td>Whatever the form allows</td>\n<td>Buttons, sensors</td>\n<td>Tangible; no app needed</td>\n<td>Hard to change after shipping; cost of hardware</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Hardware-software integration.</strong> Where a company makes the device and the software, the design can use sensors, battery and processing together: a watch that senses heart rate and also tells the user what to do about it. Questions to ask: what does the hardware sense, what can be computed on the device (which helps privacy and speed), what the battery allows, how it works with the user's other devices, and what cannot be changed after the product ships. A mistake in software can be patched; a mistake in hardware often cannot (C17.3).</p>\n<p><strong>Continuity.</strong> Many problems span devices: start on a phone, continue on a watch or speaker. Mention the handoff and what stays consistent.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use the lens.</strong> If the prompt does not name a device, choose one with a reason, and spend one sentence on it.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Regional Lens: India.</strong> Design for the phone most people carry. StatCounter's measure of mobile web use put Android at about 94% of India's mobile page views in September 2026, with iOS at about 6% <code>[V: StatCounter Global Stats, mobile operating system share in India, September 2026; it counts page views, not devices]</code>. Test a design on a mid-range and a low-end Android phone and on a throttled connection, keep payloads small, and offer the main flow in more than one language (C06). In some households one phone is shared, which changes sign-in, privacy and notification choices <code>Inferred</code>. Treat these as prompts for your clarifying questions, not facts about any one user, and read the current figures yourself at the time you read this book <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c07-c07-11-worked-designs-across-product-types\"></span>\n<h4>C07.11 Worked designs across product types</h4>\n<p>Section 5 gives four complete worked designs: a consumer commuting product, a wearable feature, a navigation problem in a public space, and a voice-only experience. Section 11 adds seven interview questions with full model answers, including two set on devices made by Apple and one on a maps product.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Seven frameworks, set out below. The segment scorecard (FW-C06-02), the tradeoff table (FW-C02-03), the success-metric template (FW-C05-06) and the pacing skeleton (FW-C03-07) are reused, not repeated.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c07-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C07-01 · The design answer spine · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a design prompt and 35 to 45 minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> an answer with eight visible steps: clarify, goal, user, problems, solutions, recommendation, metrics, risks.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the interviewer will answer clarifying questions and may redirect.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> structure makes the reasoning scorable and can make the answer sound mechanical.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> equal time on every step; skipping the decision; reciting the steps without adapting them to the prompt.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the commuting-product design in Worked example 1, and the full model answers in section 11.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: <strong>Clarify → Goal → User → Problems → Solutions → Recommend → Measure → Risks.</strong> The time budget is in section 4.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c07-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C07-02 · The goal ladder · Original</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a prompt and what you know of the company.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a three-rung statement: the company's purpose, the product goal in this area, and the user's goal, with a note on where they conflict.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the company's purpose can be reasoned about from public information.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a clear goal sharpens choices and can exclude interesting ideas.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a goal that is only a business metric; a goal the user would not recognize; pretending that goals never conflict.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> for a smartwatch feature, the company wants the device to be part of daily life, the product goal is regular healthy use, and the user's goal is to finish a first marathon without injury (IQ-C07-01).</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Rung</th>\n<th>Question</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Company</td>\n<td>Why would the company build this?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Product</td>\n<td>What outcome in this area should improve?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>User</td>\n<td>What is the person trying to achieve?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conflict</td>\n<td>Where might these pull apart?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c07-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C07-03 · The pain-point matrix · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a user and the stages of their task.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> five to seven pain points, each rated for frequency, severity and workaround, with the top one or two chosen and a stated reason.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can reason about the user's journey or have evidence from research.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> ranking forces a choice and rests on judgment when evidence is thin.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> vague pain points; no ranking; picking the one that suits a pet solution.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the commuting pain points in Worked example 1, ranked, with schedule unreliability chosen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: (1) list the stages of the task; (2) list what goes wrong at each; (3) rate frequency, severity and workaround; (4) pick the top one or two and say why; (5) say what evidence would change the ranking. The table is in C07.5.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c07-04\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C07-04 · The solution spectrum · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> the chosen pain point and the constraints.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> three or four solutions of different kinds (eliminate, automate, assist, reframe, connect), each with how it works, why it helps and what it costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the solution space is wider than the first idea.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> range versus depth in limited time.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> variants of one idea; solutions with no link to the pain point; technology-first ideas.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> pacing solutions for first-time marathon trainers (IQ-C07-01), and the movement nudges in Worked example 2.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The kinds are listed in C07.6.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c07-05\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C07-05 · The option evaluation grid · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> the solution options and the goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a comparison on impact on the goal, effort, risk, reach and fit, a chosen option, a phased plan, and the sacrifice stated.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> criteria can be drawn from the goal; rough ratings are enough.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a grid is quick and can imply false precision (see FW-C02-03).</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> scoring that justifies the favorite; no stated sacrifice; no phasing.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the station-navigation grid in Worked example 3.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Impact on goal</th>\n<th>Effort</th>\n<th>Risk</th>\n<th>Reach</th>\n<th>Fit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Recommendation line:</strong> &quot;I recommend B for the first version, which gives us ___ by giving up ___; next I would add ___; I would switch to A if ___.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c07-06\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C07-06 · The responsible-design check · Original</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> the chosen design.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the two or three most relevant risks from a standard list, each with a concrete design response, and a launch condition or kill criterion where needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can anticipate misuse and vulnerable users by reasoning about the product's data and reach.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> safeguards cost friction and adoption; no safeguards cost trust and safety.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a list of risks with no design response; defaults that favor growth over protection; ignoring bad actors.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the family location-sharing answer in IQ-C07-05, and the pre-mortem in C02 (Worked example 4).</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Check</th>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Typical response</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Misuse</td>\n<td>How could someone use this against another person?</td>\n<td>Consent from each person, visible indicators, easy exit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vulnerable users</td>\n<td>Children, older adults, people in unsafe situations?</td>\n<td>Protective defaults, extra controls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Privacy and consent</td>\n<td>What data, who sees it, can they decline?</td>\n<td>Minimize data, on-device processing, opt-in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Safety</td>\n<td>Could it lead to physical harm?</td>\n<td>Limit use while moving, cap intensity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wellbeing</td>\n<td>Could it encourage compulsion or anxiety?</td>\n<td>No streak shaming, quiet defaults</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Accessibility</td>\n<td>Who cannot use it?</td>\n<td>Multiple input and output modes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Failure</td>\n<td>What happens when it is wrong?</td>\n<td>Graceful fallback, clear uncertainty</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c07-07\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C07-07 · The surface and form-factor lens · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a problem and a device or context.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the constraints that the surface imposes (attention, input, sensing, battery, connectivity, privacy in public), and a design that fits them.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the surface is known or can be chosen with a reason.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> designing for one surface can fit poorly on another.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> porting a phone design to a watch; ignoring battery; forgetting what the device can sense.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the wearable movement nudges in Worked example 2, and the voice cooking assistant in Worked example 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The table is in C07.10. Steps: (1) state the surface and why; (2) list its constraints; (3) say what it makes easy; (4) design the moments around those; (5) say how it connects to the user's other devices.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-4-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>4. Answer architecture</h3>\n<span id=\"c07-the-design-answer-for-a-45-minute-round\"></span>\n<h4>The design answer, for a 45-minute round</h4>\n<p><strong>The structure (FW-C07-01):</strong> Clarify, Goal, User, Problems, Solutions, Recommend, Measure, Risks.</p>\n<p><strong>Time budget</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>What to produce</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Clarify and set the goal</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Three to five questions; a stated goal and assumptions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Choose the user</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Three or four segments; one chosen with a criterion; a two-sentence persona</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rank the problems</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Five to seven pain points; one or two chosen with reasons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Generate solutions</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Three or four solutions of different kinds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluate and recommend</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>A tradeoff grid; one recommendation; the first version as a flow; what comes next</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metrics</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Primary metric with definition, an input, guardrail, counter metric</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risks and wrap-up</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Two or three risks with design responses; what would change your mind</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slack for interviewer questions</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Pacing rules.</strong> Treat the interviewer's questions as steering; follow them and rejoin the spine. If you run behind, compress the solutions to two and the metrics to a sentence, and do not skip the recommendation. If the prompt gives a user, move four minutes from the user step to the problems and solutions.</p>\n<p><strong>What to clarify first.</strong> The product and platform, the goal, whether a user is given, and any constraint. Ask two or three, and state assumptions for the rest.</p>\n<p><strong>Where to signpost.</strong> After clarifying: &quot;I'll cover the user, their problems, solutions, a recommendation, and success measures.&quot; Before choosing a user: &quot;I'll pick a segment by pain and reach.&quot; Before recommending: &quot;My deciding criterion is X.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Where to state tradeoffs.</strong> In the user choice (what you give up), in the problem choice (what you leave), in the recommendation (the sacrifice), and in the risks (what safeguards cost).</p>\n<p><strong>How to close.</strong> Two sentences: &quot;I recommend X for [user], to solve [problem], and I'd measure success by Y. The main risk is Z, which I'd address with W; I'd change my mind if V.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c07-type-the-short-version-20-to-25-minutes\"></span>\n<h4>Type: the short version (20 to 25 minutes)</h4>\n<p>Keep every step and halve the time: one user, one problem, two solutions, a quick recommendation, one metric, one risk. The recommendation and the risk are the steps to protect.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-type-the-user-is-given\"></span>\n<h4>Type: the user is given</h4>\n<p>&quot;Design X for [user group].&quot; Use the saved minutes for deeper problems (two levels of &quot;why&quot;) and for solutions that fit the specific situation. Say what makes this user's situation different from the average user's.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-type-the-surface-is-given\"></span>\n<h4>Type: the surface is given</h4>\n<p>&quot;Design Y on a smartwatch / for voice.&quot; Spend the first problem minutes on the surface's constraints (FW-C07-07). Describe moments, not screens. Say what the device senses and what the battery and attention allow.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-common-interviewer-interventions-and-what-to-do\"></span>\n<h4>Common interviewer interventions, and what to do</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Interviewer says</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Who is the user?&quot;</td>\n<td>You skipped or blurred the user</td>\n<td>Name a specific segment and your criterion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Why that problem?&quot;</td>\n<td>Your ranking is unclear</td>\n<td>State frequency, severity and workaround</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What else could you do?&quot;</td>\n<td>They want range</td>\n<td>Offer a solution of a different kind</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;How would you know it works?&quot;</td>\n<td>They want a metric</td>\n<td>Use the C05 template</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What could go wrong?&quot;</td>\n<td>They want the downside</td>\n<td>Misuse, privacy and failure, with a response</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Let's go deeper on the first version&quot;</td>\n<td>They like it</td>\n<td>Describe the flow, edge cases and what you would cut</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c07-5-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h3>5. Worked examples</h3>\n<p>All four examples are <code>Illustrative</code>: the products are fictional and every figure is an invented assumption. Each follows the spine in FW-C07-01 and is written as notes you could say aloud. A spoken answer is shorter than these notes; the point here is to show every step and the reasoning behind each choice.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-worked-example-1-consumer-a-feature-for-people-who-commute-b\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 1 — Consumer: a feature for people who commute by public transport (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a transit-planning feature of the kind offered in maps products such as Google's. No fact about any real product is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The prompt.</strong> &quot;Design a product for people who commute by public transport.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Clarify and goal.</strong> I'd ask three things: is this a new product or a feature in an existing app, on which device, and what does the company want? Suppose the answer is &quot;a feature in an existing transit-planning app on phones, in a mid-sized city, and the company wants the app to be part of the daily commute.&quot; The goal ladder (FW-C07-02): the company wants daily use; the product goal is that a commute goes the way the traveler planned; the user's goal is to arrive on time without wasting time. Conflict to watch: more notifications raise usage and lower trust.</p>\n<p><strong>User.</strong> Three segments by need and behavior: daily commuters with a fixed arrival time and at least one transfer; occasional riders such as visitors and event-goers; commuters with flexible hours. I choose the first. Pain intensity is high (a late arrival has a cost), the trips repeat about ten times a week, the app already reaches them, and the company's real-time data fits their problem. I give up occasional riders, who are numerous but whose trips carry little at stake, and flexible commuters, who feel the pain less. Persona: Dana travels forty minutes each way with one transfer and has a 9:00 stand-up (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Pain points (FW-C07-03).</strong> Walking the journey in stages:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pain point</th>\n<th>Frequency</th>\n<th>Severity</th>\n<th>Workaround today</th>\n<th>Rank</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Not knowing until she reaches the platform whether her usual train is late</td>\n<td>About 3 trips in 10 are delayed by five minutes or more (invented)</td>\n<td>High when a transfer is at risk</td>\n<td>Checks the app several times; leaves 15 minutes early every day</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Missing a tight transfer after a delay</td>\n<td>About 1 trip in 10 (invented)</td>\n<td>High: a 15-minute extra wait</td>\n<td>Takes an earlier train than needed</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Crowded platform at peak</td>\n<td>Daily</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Stands at a different platform end</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Getting from the station to the door</td>\n<td>Daily</td>\n<td>Low to medium</td>\n<td>Walks; sometimes late</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Finding a seat</td>\n<td>Daily</td>\n<td>Low to medium</td>\n<td>Stands at the usual door</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fare and ticket friction</td>\n<td>Weekly</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Monthly pass</td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Boredom</td>\n<td>Daily</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Podcasts</td>\n<td>7</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>I choose the first two, which share a cause: the traveler cannot tell in time whether to leave earlier. The workaround is the evidence. Leaving 15 minutes early on about 21 working days is roughly five hours a month (invented), paid to guard against a delay that happens on three trips in ten. I'd verify the frequencies in interviews and the app's data, and I'd expect them to rank above crowding and seats.</p>\n<p><strong>Solutions that differ in kind (FW-C07-04).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Solution</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Eliminate</td>\n<td>Hold the connection</td>\n<td>The operator holds the connecting train when a feeder train is late. Outside the app's control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Automate</td>\n<td>Reroute and rebook</td>\n<td>When a delay is detected, the app picks another route and rebooks the ticket</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assist</td>\n<td>Latest-safe-departure alert</td>\n<td>&quot;Leave by 8:12 to make your 9:00.&quot; The time updates as delays develop</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reframe</td>\n<td>Plan by arrival window</td>\n<td>The traveler sets &quot;be there by 9:00&quot; and the app chooses the train, instead of the traveler choosing a train</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Connect</td>\n<td>Rider delay reports</td>\n<td>Riders on the same train report conditions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Evaluation (FW-C07-05).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Impact on goal</th>\n<th>Effort</th>\n<th>Risk</th>\n<th>Reach</th>\n<th>Fit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Hold the connection</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>High (needs the operator)</td>\n<td>High (not ours to control)</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Reroute and rebook</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Medium to high (wrong reroute, payment)</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Latest-safe-departure alert</td>\n<td>Medium to high</td>\n<td>Low to medium (uses the existing real-time feed)</td>\n<td>Low to medium (false alarms)</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>High</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D. Arrival-window planner</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium (changes a habit)</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium to high</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E. Rider delay reports</td>\n<td>Low to medium</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium (spam; thin data early)</td>\n<td>Low until density builds</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> &quot;I recommend C for the first version, which gives commuters their buffer time back by giving up automatic rerouting; next I would add rerouting for the cases where the alert is followed and the transfer is still missed; I would switch to B sooner if interviews show that people want the app to decide for them.&quot; The deadline the user sets (from D) is the one input C needs, so C borrows the useful part of D.</p>\n<p>The first version as moments: (1) One-time setup: the user enters the usual route and the arrival deadline. (2) On a weekday at 7:52 a quiet notification says &quot;Leave by 8:12; your train is running four minutes late and your transfer still looks safe.&quot; (3) If the transfer is at risk, the message changes to &quot;Leave by 8:05, or take the 8:20 via Central.&quot; (4) One tap starts the trip. (5) On arrival, a single optional tap: &quot;Did this help?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Metrics (FW-C05-06, compact).</strong> Primary: the share of commute days on which the user's trip ends by the deadline they set, for alert users against a holdout group, so the metric compares like with like (the trip-end signal comes from the app's own trip tracking, which avoids needing continuous location). Input: the share of alerts followed by a departure within five minutes of the suggested time. <a href=\"#gl-guardrail-metric\">guardrail metric</a>: false-alarm rate (alerts that told the user to leave earlier when the train ran on time), below 10% (invented threshold), and a monthly notification-mute rate below 5% (invented). <a href=\"#gl-counter-metric\">counter metric</a>: the average buffer users add, because if it grows, the alert may be teaching people to over-leave.</p>\n<p><strong>Risks.</strong> Location history can reveal home and work, so commute patterns stay on the device. Alert fatigue destroys trust fast, so alerts fire only when the recommended departure time changes by more than a few minutes. When the real-time feed is wrong the alert is wrong, so the message states the confidence (&quot;looks safe,&quot; &quot;tight&quot;) rather than a false certainty. I would change my mind if the holdout comparison showed no difference in on-time arrivals, since then the alert would be informing people without helping them.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-worked-example-2-wearable-a-smartwatch-feature-to-help-desk-\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 2 — Wearable: a smartwatch feature to help desk workers move more (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a smartwatch feature of the kind Apple and several other companies make. No fact about any real product is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The prompt.</strong> &quot;Design a smartwatch feature that helps desk workers move more during the day.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Clarify and goal.</strong> The feature is in an existing watch and phone ecosystem. The watch senses motion and heart rate, and can read the calendar with the wearer's permission. Goal ladder: the company wants the watch to be worn and used daily; the product goal is more regular movement breaks; the user's goal is to feel better by the end of the workday without losing focus. Conflict: every extra buzz that raises engagement costs trust, and the user also wants fewer interruptions in meetings.</p>\n<p><strong>User.</strong> By behavior: workers in back-to-back meetings, who sit for two to three hours at a stretch without noticing; deep-focus workers, who sit long by choice and resent interruptions; and hybrid workers with flexible days. I choose the first. Their sitting is structural (the calendar causes it), predictable (the product can see the blocks), and they cannot easily interrupt themselves in a meeting. I give up deep-focus workers, for whom an interruption is costly and the benefit is smaller. Persona: Sam has meetings from 9:00 to 12:30 most days, with no gap (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Pain points.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pain point</th>\n<th>Frequency</th>\n<th>Severity</th>\n<th>Workaround today</th>\n<th>Rank</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sitting through a long meeting block without noticing</td>\n<td>Daily</td>\n<td>Medium (stiffness, fatigue)</td>\n<td>Takes calls standing when possible</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A generic hourly &quot;stand up&quot; prompt arrives mid-presentation</td>\n<td>Daily</td>\n<td>Medium (embarrassing, then ignored)</td>\n<td>Disables the reminder</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No gap in the calendar to move</td>\n<td>Several days a week</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Declines meetings</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The prompt says &quot;stand&quot; and the wearer cannot</td>\n<td>Rare overall; for those affected, every time</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Turns the feature off</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Feeling judged by streaks and rings</td>\n<td>Weekly</td>\n<td>Medium (guilt)</td>\n<td>Ignores the watch</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Forgetting after returning to the desk</td>\n<td>Daily</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>None</td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The first two are one problem seen from two sides: reminders do not know what the person is doing, so they arrive when the person cannot act on them. The fourth is rare in the population and severe for the people it affects, so it becomes a design requirement rather than a ranked problem: the wording and the options must work for everyone (the accessibility row in FW-C07-06).</p>\n<p><strong>Surface lens (FW-C07-07).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Constraint</th>\n<th>What it means here</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Attention</td>\n<td>Three seconds. One tap and one line of text</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Input</td>\n<td>A tap or a dismiss; no typing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sensing</td>\n<td>Wrist motion shows long stillness; the calendar shows meeting blocks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Battery</td>\n<td>Motion sensing is cheap; continuous heart-rate and location sampling are expensive and not needed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Privacy</td>\n<td>Calendar content stays on the device; only free-or-busy is used</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Continuity</td>\n<td>The phone holds settings and history; the watch acts in the moment</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Solutions that differ in kind.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Solution</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Eliminate</td>\n<td>Suggest that meetings end five minutes early, as a prompt to the person who organizes them (a calendar change, not a watch feature)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Automate</td>\n<td>Detect and credit movement automatically, with no prompt at all</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assist</td>\n<td>A tap at the end of a long meeting block with &quot;Two minutes to stretch or walk?&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reframe</td>\n<td>Count any movement, such as a walk to the printer, rather than only standing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Connect</td>\n<td>Invite a colleague to a short walk</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> &quot;I recommend the end-of-block tap, with the reframe that any movement counts. It gives the wearer a prompt at a moment they can act on, by giving up the simplicity of a fixed hourly schedule. Next I'd add a weekly view of which time slots they accept, and later the colleague invitation. I'd pick automatic crediting alone if people turn out to want no prompts.&quot;</p>\n<p>The first version as moments: (1) A meeting block ends and the watch has sensed about 90 minutes of stillness (invented). (2) One tap on the wrist and the line &quot;Two minutes to stretch or walk?&quot; with &quot;Later&quot; and &quot;Not today.&quot; (3) If the watch records two minutes of movement in the next ten minutes, a quiet confirmation tap follows. (4) After three dismissals for the same time slot, the nudge stops for that slot and asks once whether to change it.</p>\n<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> Primary: the share of nudges followed within ten minutes by at least two minutes of recorded movement. Input: the share of nudges delivered while the wearer is not in a meeting, which should be above 95% (invented). Guardrails: the monthly rate at which wearers turn the feature off, below 3% (invented); the battery impact, below 1% of a day's charge (invented). Counter metric: the share of nudges dismissed within a second, which signals annoyance even when the feature stays on.</p>\n<p><strong>Risks.</strong> Wellbeing: no streak shaming, no red &quot;failed&quot; states, and quiet defaults. Accessibility: the wording says &quot;move&quot; or &quot;stretch&quot; rather than &quot;stand,&quot; and the wearer can set a seated mode. Privacy: calendar content never leaves the device. I'd change my mind if the movement-following rate were high but the disable rate also rose, since that would mean the feature works for some people and costs others.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-worked-example-3-public-space-helping-travelers-change-train\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 3 — Public space: helping travelers change trains in an unfamiliar station (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The prompt.</strong> &quot;Design a product that helps people find their way around a large train station.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Clarify and goal.</strong> I'd ask whether the product is an app on a phone and whether the station operator is a partner. Assume a phone app from a ticketing company, no control over the station's physical layout, and a goal of making the ticket app useful on the day of travel. Goal ladder: the company wants the app to matter at the moment of travel; the product goal is that travelers reach their platform in time; the user's goal is to make the connection without stress. Conflict: the app cannot change the station, only what the traveler knows.</p>\n<p><strong>User.</strong> By need: first-time transfer travelers with a 10 to 15 minute connection and luggage; regular commuters who know the station; travelers who need a step-free route (wheelchair, stroller, heavy bags); visitors who are leaving the station for the city. I choose the first. The stakes are a missed connection, the situation is unfamiliar and time-limited, and a ticketing app reaches them before they arrive. The step-free traveler is an extreme user (see the mental models below): their needs are sharper versions of everyone's, so I'd design the route so that a step-free option exists from the start, and treat their segment as the next one, not as an afterthought. I give up regular commuters, who need nothing, and visitors, who have time.</p>\n<p><strong>Pain points.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pain point</th>\n<th>Frequency</th>\n<th>Severity</th>\n<th>Workaround today</th>\n<th>Rank</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>The way from the arrival platform to the departure platform is unclear when the traveler gets off</td>\n<td>Every transfer</td>\n<td>High (minutes matter)</td>\n<td>Follows the crowd; asks staff</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The train stops at the far end from the stairs to the next platform</td>\n<td>Often (invented)</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Runs</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Positioning fails indoors, so a map dot jumps or freezes</td>\n<td>Every indoor use</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Gives up on the map</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Signs assume the traveler already knows the platform number</td>\n<td>Often</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Waits for the board</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Step-free routes are hard to find</td>\n<td>For those who need them, every time</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Asks staff; takes the long way</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Platform changes are announced late</td>\n<td>Occasional</td>\n<td>High when it happens</td>\n<td>Listens to announcements</td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>I choose the first two, which share a cause: the traveler has to solve a navigation problem at the moment of arrival, with no knowledge of where the exit is relative to where the train stopped.</p>\n<p><strong>Solutions that differ in kind.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Solution</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Eliminate</td>\n<td>Better signs and earlier platform announcements (the operator's action; I'd recommend it, not build it)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Automate</td>\n<td>Indoor turn-by-turn with beacons, rerouting live as the traveler moves</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assist</td>\n<td>An offline route in landmarks and photos (&quot;up the stairs, turn left past the bakery&quot;), loaded when the ticket is bought</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reframe</td>\n<td>Tell the traveler, at the first station, which part of the train to board so they get off nearest the stairs to the next platform</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Connect</td>\n<td>Booked or on-demand help from station staff, mainly for step-free travelers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Evaluation.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Impact on goal</th>\n<th>Effort</th>\n<th>Risk</th>\n<th>Reach</th>\n<th>Fit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Signs and announcements</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Not ours</td>\n<td>n/a</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Indoor turn-by-turn</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>High (beacons per station)</td>\n<td>Medium to high (outages, wrong dot)</td>\n<td>Low at first (few stations)</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Offline landmark route</td>\n<td>Medium to high</td>\n<td>Low to medium (one route per station pair)</td>\n<td>Low (static, works with no signal)</td>\n<td>Medium to high</td>\n<td>High</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D. Where to board</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Medium (needs train formation data)</td>\n<td>Medium (the data can change)</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>High</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E. Staff help</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Low to medium</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> &quot;I recommend D together with C for the first version, for the busiest stations, which gives travelers a short, reliable route and a shorter walk by giving up live positioning. Next I'd add step-free versions of each route, and then live indoor guidance in stations that install beacons. I'd bring B forward if user tests showed that people do not trust a static route.&quot; The two pieces reinforce each other: boarding at the right place shortens the route, and the route tells them what to do when they step off.</p>\n<p>The first version as moments: (1) At purchase, the app downloads the transfer guide for this journey, since stations often have poor signal. (2) At the origin, a message says &quot;Board the middle carriages; they stop beside the stairs for platform 9.&quot; (3) On arrival, the screen shows three photos and one line each. (4) A &quot;step-free route&quot; switch shows the alternative with its extra time. (5) If the platform changes, a notification replaces the route and says so.</p>\n<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> Primary: the share of transfers completed with at least three minutes to spare among travelers who opened the guide, compared with a staggered rollout across stations rather than comparing users who chose to open it with those who did not (the first group would differ in motivation). Input: the share of guide users who open the boarding advice before the first leg. Guardrails: the rate at which the boarding advice is wrong, because train formations change, below 5% (invented); and offline load success, above 99% (invented). Counter metric: phone use while walking at the station, because a guide that makes people stare at a screen creates a safety problem; the design answers it with large type and short audio cues.</p>\n<p><strong>Risks.</strong> Failure: if formation data is uncertain, the app shows the general route and says it is unsure, and does not give a confident wrong carriage. Safety: glance-only design and no demand to hold the phone up. Accessibility: the step-free route is built in, not added later. I'd change my mind if tests showed that the traveler's problem was mainly announcements arriving late, which would be an operator conversation and not a product.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-worked-example-4-voice-a-cooking-assistant-with-no-screen-il\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 4 — Voice: a cooking assistant with no screen (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The prompt.</strong> &quot;Design a voice-only assistant that helps people cook from a recipe.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Clarify and goal.</strong> The surface is given: a smart speaker in the kitchen. Goal ladder: the company wants the speaker to be useful in a daily routine; the product goal is that cooks finish recipes by voice; the user's goal is to make the meal without wrecking it or their phone. Conflict: the speaker is always listening in the home, so trust is part of the product.</p>\n<p><strong>User.</strong> By situation: cooks following a recipe for the first time (hands busy, high attention); experienced cooks who use a recipe as a rough guide; people cooking for a group on a deadline. I choose the first. They need the most help, they follow steps in order, and their hands are messy, which is exactly when a screen fails. I give up experienced cooks, who want less guidance, and group cooks, whose problems include coordinating dishes. Persona: Lee is making lentil soup for the first time with wet hands, with the extractor fan on (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Pain points</strong> (reasoned from the situation; I'd confirm by watching someone cook).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pain point</th>\n<th>Frequency</th>\n<th>Severity</th>\n<th>Workaround today</th>\n<th>Rank</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Losing the place in the recipe after an interruption</td>\n<td>Most cooking sessions</td>\n<td>High (a missed step spoils the dish)</td>\n<td>Re-reads the whole page; puts a finger on the step</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Touching a phone screen with messy hands</td>\n<td>Every session</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Wipes hands; the screen times out</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Running two timers and forgetting which is which</td>\n<td>Several recipes a week</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>A kitchen timer plus memory</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Not knowing the ingredients are all there before starting</td>\n<td>Often</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Reads the list at the start and again</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unclear steps (&quot;cook until done&quot;)</td>\n<td>Often</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Searches for a video</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>I choose the first three, which are all problems of memory and hands.</p>\n<p><strong>What voice changes (FW-C07-07).</strong> No screen means every output must be said, so it must be short and in a sensible order. Speech recognition fails in noise, so important actions need a confirmation. The speaker cannot see the cook, so it cannot tell whether a step is done. Users cannot scan a spoken list, so the assistant reads in small chunks. The cook cannot say &quot;tap,&quot; but can say &quot;next,&quot; &quot;repeat,&quot; &quot;go back&quot; and &quot;how long?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Solutions that differ in kind.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Solution</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Eliminate</td>\n<td>The assistant holds the place: it knows which step the cook is on, so interruptions cannot lose it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Automate</td>\n<td>Named timers started automatically from the recipe (&quot;pasta, nine minutes&quot;)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assist</td>\n<td>Read the ingredients three at a time and ask for a &quot;ready&quot; before the first step</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reframe</td>\n<td>Describe the cue instead of the time (&quot;until the onions are soft and clear&quot;)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Connect</td>\n<td>Hand off to a video call with a relative who knows the recipe</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> &quot;I recommend the place-holding assistant with named timers and cue-based steps for the first version, which solves the memory problems by giving up recipe discovery and any visual content. Next I'd add the ingredient check and pantry awareness. I'd test a screen companion if cooks keep asking to see the steps.&quot; The central design decision is that the assistant holds state, so the cook never has to.</p>\n<p>A moment of the first version (invented dialogue):</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Lee:</strong> &quot;Start the lentil soup.&quot; <strong>Assistant:</strong> &quot;Lentil soup: six steps, about forty minutes. Eight ingredients; I'll read three at a time. Say 'ready' when you have them.&quot; ... <strong>Lee:</strong> &quot;How long is left on the onions?&quot; <strong>Assistant:</strong> &quot;Onion timer: four minutes. The soup timer starts at step four.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>When a timer is set, the assistant confirms in plain words (&quot;Pasta timer, nine minutes, started&quot;) so that a mishearing is caught immediately. &quot;Next,&quot; &quot;repeat&quot; and &quot;go back&quot; always work, and after a long silence the assistant says nothing, because it cannot tell silence from a busy cook.</p>\n<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> Primary: the share of started recipes completed by voice alone, without the user switching to a phone or a screen mid-recipe. Input: the share of sessions in which the cook uses &quot;repeat&quot; or &quot;where was I,&quot; which shows that the state-holding is being used. Guardrails: recognition-error rate per session (&quot;Sorry, I didn't get that&quot;), and timer accuracy. Counter metric: abandonment mid-recipe, which could rise if the assistant is more annoying than a printed page.</p>\n<p><strong>Risks.</strong> Privacy: the speaker listens in the home, so the wake word is the only trigger, audio stays on the device until it is spoken, and there is a visible indicator and a mute control. Safety: the assistant gives times and cues, never claims food is safe to eat (&quot;chicken is cooked&quot;) from time alone, and says to check with a thermometer. Accessibility: speech-impaired or accented speakers may be misrecognized, so a simple fallback (a button on the speaker) matters. I'd change my mind if the testing showed that cooks wanted to see the steps, which would move this to a screen-and-voice product.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-6-baseline-strong-standout\"></span>\n<h3>6. Baseline, Strong, Standout</h3>\n<p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;Design a product that helps older adults stay in touch with their families.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c07-baseline-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Baseline answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I'd build an app for seniors with a simple interface. It would have video calls, photo sharing and big buttons. I'd make the font large and add reminders to call family.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What is wrong:</em> &quot;older adults&quot; is a demographic, not a user. There is no problem, no ranking and no alternative to the first idea. The design assumes the person has a phone and wants an app. There is no decision, no metric, and nothing on risk.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-strong-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Strong answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I'd first ask whether this is a new product and who the company is, and assume a new product from a company with consumer hardware and software experience, and a goal of strengthening family contact. 'Older adults' includes people who are active with smartphones and people who have never used one. I'd segment by situation: those who live alone and are comfortable with a phone, those living alone who are not, and those in care homes. I'd choose people who live alone and are not comfortable with phones, because they are most at risk of isolation and are least served by current products.</p>\n<p>&quot;Their problems, ranked by how often and how costly: starting a call is hard (several steps and small buttons), they miss calls because the phone is in another room, family members cannot tell when it is a good time to call, and settings go wrong and nobody can fix them remotely. I'd pick starting and receiving a call, because it happens every day and it is the root of contact.</p>\n<p>&quot;Solutions that differ in kind: a dedicated device with one button per family member; an app on the person's existing phone with a simplified home screen; a family-managed service that sets everything up from the family's phone; and a scheduled weekly call with reminders. I'd recommend the dedicated device for the first version, since it removes the learning, and I'd measure success by the number of two-way calls per week between each pair. I'd flag risks: cost and setup, and scams.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c07-standout-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Standout answer</h4>\n<p>Everything in the Strong answer, plus:</p>\n<p>&quot;The buyer and the user are different people. The adult child buys and sets it up, and the older parent uses it. So there are two users with different goals and I'd design for both: the parent needs calls to be effortless and respectful, and the adult child needs to know it works without checking on the parent. That difference decides the setup design: the child configures it remotely, and the parent never sees a settings screen.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd watch for the dignity problem. A product that reports on the parent's activity (when they woke, whether they moved) can feel like surveillance. So any status sharing is the parent's choice, shown to them, and off by default, and I'd say that out loud because it is the line between a connection product and a monitoring product.</p>\n<p>&quot;The most serious risk is that older adults are frequent targets of scam calls. A device that makes calling easy also makes receiving calls easy, so by default only approved contacts ring through, and unknown callers go to a quiet inbox that the family can review with the parent's permission.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd choose the dedicated device partly because of the surface. A person with no smartphone habit has nothing to learn with a button, but a hardware device is hard to change after shipping, so I'd run the first version as a prototype with a few families before committing to manufacturing.</p>\n<p>&quot;Metric: weekly two-way contact per pair, not minutes of use, since a longer call that the parent finds tiring is not a win. A counter metric is the parent's reported sense of obligation, which I'd check in short interviews. I'd change my mind if parents turned out to want the call on their own phone, in which case the product is a simplified layer and not a device.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c07-what-moves-an-answer-up-a-band\"></span>\n<h4>What moves an answer up a band</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From Baseline to Strong</th>\n<th>From Strong to Standout</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Replaces a demographic with a situation and a criterion</td>\n<td>Separates the buyer from the user and designs for both</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ranks problems and picks one</td>\n<td>Names the dignity and consent line and the main misuse risk (scams), with a default that answers it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Offers solutions of different kinds and recommends one</td>\n<td>Links the surface choice to the problem and flags what cannot be changed after shipping</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gives a metric</td>\n<td>Chooses a metric that cannot be gamed by longer or more frequent use, and says what would change the recommendation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c07-7-company-lens\"></span>\n<h3>7. Company lens</h3>\n<p>All claims describe what companies say publicly or what candidates commonly report, not insider knowledge. Interview formats change <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Google [as of 2026-10].</strong> Google has long published a stated philosophy that starts with focusing on the user <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Google, Ten things we know to be true]</code>, and it publishes design guidance for its products <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Material Design and Google's published design principles (listing on principles.design)]</code>. Candidates commonly report product-design prompts that use Google's own products, often at consumer scale and across many countries <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: choose a specific user, and say how the design would work for people with older phones, poor connections or different languages. Appendix A has briefs on eight Google products to practice against.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Amazon [as of 2026-10].</strong> Amazon publishes Customer Obsession as a leadership principle <code>Public</code>, and former leaders describe writing a press release and a list of customer questions before building, known as working backwards <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon working-backwards material (AWS re:Invent 2022 deck; Fast Company)]</code>. Candidates commonly report that design answers are expected to start from the customer and a concrete problem <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: say the customer's problem in the customer's words, and be ready to write the one-paragraph announcement that would make that customer care.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Meta [as of 2026-10].</strong> Meta publishes a mission centered on connecting people <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Meta careers page: build the future of how people connect]</code>. Candidates commonly report that product-sense rounds expect a specific user, a quantified problem, and follow-up questions on how you would know the design works and on risks such as safety and misuse <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: pair each user claim with a way to size it, and prepare a real answer for misuse of any feature that connects people.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Microsoft [as of 2026-10].</strong> Microsoft publishes inclusive-design principles and a toolkit <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Microsoft Inclusive Design toolkit manual (Microsoft Design)]</code>, and its products often serve a buyer, an administrator and a daily user who are different people <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: say whose problem you are solving, and treat people with permanent, temporary and situational limits as a source of design ideas.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Apple [as of 2026-10].</strong> Apple publishes human-interface guidelines for its platforms and a public position on privacy <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Apple Human Interface Guidelines (developer.apple.com) and privacy pages]</code>, and it designs hardware and software together <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Apple's own description of its platforms and services]</code>. Third-party guides report that design prompts at Apple often involve its own devices and weigh craft and fit with the user's other devices <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: name what the hardware senses and what stays on the device, describe moments rather than screens, and say how the design works with the user's other devices.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Netflix [as of 2026-10].</strong> Netflix's published culture material emphasizes judgment and context <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Netflix culture memo, June 2024]</code>. Candidates commonly report design prompts about personalization and viewing habits that expect a behavioral measure of success <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: tie each design to a behavior you can observe, and say what you would test before building.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Stripe [as of 2026-10].</strong> Stripe publishes a mission about growing online commerce and is widely described as emphasizing documentation and developer experience <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Stripe newsroom (mission to grow the GDP of the internet); the documentation reputation is widely described and not sourced]</code>. Prompts for developer-facing products, where the user writes code or integrates a service, are reported by candidates <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: when the user is a developer, the product includes its documentation, errors and first integration, and the first five minutes decide adoption.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c07-8-common-mistakes\"></span>\n<h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>How to detect it</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Designing before choosing a user</td>\n<td>Your first sentence describes a feature</td>\n<td>State a segment and a criterion first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>A demographic user</td>\n<td>&quot;Busy parents,&quot; &quot;seniors,&quot; &quot;students&quot;</td>\n<td>Segment by situation and need</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>A long, unranked list of problems</td>\n<td>You cannot say which one the solutions address</td>\n<td>Rank by frequency, severity and workaround; pick one or two</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>A vague pain point</td>\n<td>You could not watch it happen</td>\n<td>Write it as something observable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Solutions that are variants of one idea</td>\n<td>They differ only in size or polish</td>\n<td>Offer different kinds: eliminate, automate, assist, reframe, connect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Technology first</td>\n<td>&quot;Use AI to...&quot; before naming the problem</td>\n<td>State the problem, then say why the technology is the right tool and what happens when it fails</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Describing screens</td>\n<td>You are talking about buttons and layouts</td>\n<td>Describe moments: what the user does, the product does, the user feels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>No decision</td>\n<td>You end on &quot;there are tradeoffs&quot;</td>\n<td>Recommend one option and name the sacrifice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>A generic metric</td>\n<td>&quot;Daily active users&quot; for a feature built to solve one problem</td>\n<td>Tie the primary metric to the chosen problem, with a guardrail and a counter metric</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Ignoring misuse, privacy and accessibility</td>\n<td>Your risks are all about adoption</td>\n<td>Name two or three harms for this product and a design response for each</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c07-9-mental-models-applied\"></span>\n<h3>9. Mental models applied</h3>\n<p>Four models, each tied to a decision in this chapter. <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a> and <a href=\"#gl-second-order-effect\">second-order effect</a> come from C02, and extreme users from C06.</p>\n<p><strong>Inversion</strong> (<code>MM-inversion</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a>, C02). Ask how you would design the product to fail its user: choose a user nobody can picture, solve a problem that is easy to describe but rare, use a technology that works only in the lab, ignore what the device cannot sense. The answers are the checklist for a good design. It is especially useful in the risks step: &quot;How would someone use this against another person?&quot; is inversion applied to a feature.</p>\n<p><strong>Extreme users</strong> (<code>MM-extreme-users</code>, from C06). Designing for people at the edges of a segment exposes needs that typical users have more mildly. A traveler in a wheelchair finds the step-free route that everyone with a stroller or heavy bags also needs; a person who is deaf needs an alarm that does not depend on sound, and a heavy sleeper benefits from it too. Use the extreme user as a source of ideas and constraints, and test them with typical users before deciding.</p>\n<p><strong>The curb-cut effect</strong> (<code>MM-curb-cut</code>). A design made for a constraint often benefits everyone. Ramps cut into curbs for wheelchair users also serve people with strollers, suitcases and bicycles. In an interview this is a way to justify an accessible default as a design strength and not a cost: captions help people in noisy places, large targets help people walking, and voice control helps people with wet hands.</p>\n<p><strong>Defaults decide behavior</strong> (<code>MM-defaults</code>). Defaults have a large effect on what people end up with, so for many users the default is the product <code>[V: Johnson and Goldstein, &quot;Do Defaults Save Lives?&quot;, Science 302, 2003: far higher effective consent to organ donation where the default is opt-out; these are consent rates, not donations]</code>. In a design answer, the default is where the privacy, safety and wellbeing choices become real: sharing is off until the person opts in, alerts are quiet until they ask for more, a seated mode is easy to find. Say what the default is, and who it protects. A default that favors engagement over the person is a design choice, and an interviewer can ask you to defend it.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-engineering-design-and-data-science-perspectives\"></span>\n<h3>Engineering, design and data-science perspectives</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engineering.</strong> Ask early what the device can sense, what can be computed on the device, and what cannot be changed after release. A hardware choice made in week two can fix a constraint for years; a software choice can be patched. Bring an engineer into the problem step, not only the solution step, because they will often know a cheaper way to reach the user's goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Design and research.</strong> A designer will turn a moment into a flow and show you where it breaks. Ask for the three screens that must be perfect and the ones that can be rough, and ask a researcher what evidence would change the ranking of the pain points. A design review is a place to defend the problem, not only the solution.</li>\n<li><strong>Data science.</strong> Ask what data exists to size the pain points, and what you would need to instrument to measure the primary metric (C05). Tell the data scientist the decision the metric supports, and agree on the counter metric before launch, so that a good-looking number does not decide by default. Measurement design for the experiment itself is covered in C14.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c07-checklist-before-you-say-i-recommend\"></span>\n<h3>Checklist: before you say &quot;I recommend&quot;</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I choose a specific user by a stated criterion, and say what I gave up?</li>\n<li>Is the problem written as something observable, ranked against others, with a reason?</li>\n<li>Do my solutions differ in kind, and does each link back to the problem?</li>\n<li>Did I name the deciding criterion before comparing the options?</li>\n<li>Is the first version described as moments, with what comes next in order?</li>\n<li>Does the primary metric match the problem, with a guardrail and a counter metric?</li>\n<li>Did I name two or three harms for this product, each with a design response and a default?</li>\n<li>Did I say what would change my mind?</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c07-10-case-studies\"></span>\n<h3>10. Case studies</h3>\n<span id=\"cs-c07-01\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C07-01 · The bike-share first ride · Illustrative · Beginner</h5>\n<p><em>Fictional composite. Every number is invented (invented).</em></p>\n";
